## 3. When to Use What — A Decision Guide

This section helps you decide which approach fits your use case, and which tool to use to build it.

### Should this be a Skill, a Workflow, or an Agent?

| Use Case Characteristics | Recommended Approach |
|---|---|
| Single, well-defined task with clear input/output | **Skill** |
| Multiple steps, but the sequence is fixed and known in advance | **Workflow** |
| Multiple steps, where what happens next depends on intermediate results | **Agent** |
| Requires calling external systems (databases, APIs, file stores) | **Agent** (with tool access) |
| Needs a human to review or approve before proceeding | **Agent or Workflow** with human-in-the-loop step |
| High-frequency, repeatable, low-risk | **Skill or Workflow** |
| Complex, variable, higher-stakes | **Agent** with appropriate guardrails |

When in doubt, start simpler. A workflow that calls two or three skills is easier to test, audit, and fix than a fully autonomous agent. Move to agents only when the task genuinely requires adaptive decision-making.

### Which Tool Should I Use?

DBS Finance currently has access to the following AI surfaces. Each has a different sweet spot.

---

**DBS GPT**
DBS's internal AI assistant, broadly similar to ChatGPT. It has a UI that supports building simple workflows (chains of prompts and steps) without writing code.

*Best for:* Quick drafting, summarisation, one-off analysis, simple workflow automation where the steps are fixed and the data is not sensitive in ways that require additional controls.

*Limitations:* The underlying model is not the most capable available. Workflow-building capability is functional but limited in complexity. Not well-suited for multi-step adaptive tasks or integration with Finance-specific systems.

*When to choose it:* When you need something fast, the task is relatively simple, and you want a no-code interface. Good for early prototyping.

---

**Microsoft Copilot Studio**
Microsoft's tool for building AI-powered workflows and agents, with stronger integration into the Microsoft 365 ecosystem (Teams, SharePoint, Excel, Outlook).

*Best for:* Workflows that are anchored in Microsoft tools — for example, triggering an AI step when a file is uploaded to SharePoint, or drafting a Teams message based on structured data in Excel.

*Limitations:* Similarly constrained on model quality. Can feel clunky outside the Microsoft ecosystem. Building complex, Finance-specific logic requires care to avoid fragile workflows.

*When to choose it:* When your workflow lives primarily in Microsoft tools and the value is in the integration (not the AI reasoning itself).

---

**Claude Code** *(coming soon)*
An AI coding assistant that operates in your development environment. It can read and write code, run commands, interact with files, and work through multi-step technical tasks with significant autonomy.

*Best for:* Developers building Finance AI capabilities — writing skill logic, building agent scaffolding, automating data pipelines, writing test cases. This is a productivity tool for people who write code, not an end-user workflow tool.

*When to choose it:* When you are building or maintaining code that powers Finance AI capabilities. Not intended as a business user tool.

---

**Claude Cowork** *(coming soon)*
A desktop AI tool aimed at non-developers that can automate file and task management across your machine. Think of it as an AI assistant that can work across your local tools — not just within a single app.

*Best for:* Business users who want to automate repetitive file-handling, document processing, or cross-application tasks without writing code.

*When to choose it:* When the task involves working across files and local tools and you need something more capable and flexible than DBS GPT or Copilot Studio.

---

**A practical note on tool choice:** Today, DBS GPT and Copilot Studio are the sanctioned, production-ready options. Claude Code and Cowork are on the horizon and will require appropriate access and onboarding when available. Do not use unsanctioned AI tools for Finance workflows — both for data security reasons and because outputs from such tools cannot be audited or governed.

