## 5. Tool and Data Access Guidelines

Agents and workflows in Finance often need to access data — from internal systems, shared drives, databases, or APIs. Access must be appropriate, controlled, and auditable.

### Data Classification

Before building any agent or workflow that touches data, confirm the data classification of everything it will access or produce.

| Data Type | Examples | Access Requirement |
|---|---|---|
| Public / Internal Use | Published reports, general reference data | Standard access; no special approval needed |
| Confidential | Budget data, counterparty information, management accounts | Access must be justified and logged |
| Restricted / Regulated | Customer data, trade data subject to MAS regulations | Requires explicit approval from data owner and compliance sign-off before use in any AI workflow |

Do not feed Restricted data into any AI tool unless it has been specifically approved for that data classification. When in doubt, check with your data steward or the AI Hub team before proceeding.

### Tool Access Principles

When an agent is given access to a tool (for example, the ability to query a database or call an internal API), the following principles apply:

**Least privilege.** The agent should only have access to the data and actions it needs for the specific task — nothing more. Do not grant broad access on the assumption it might be useful later.

**Read before write.** Agents should default to reading and drafting. Any agent that writes to a system, sends a communication, or takes an action with external effect must have a human approval step before that action is taken. No autonomous writes to production systems without explicit sign-off on this design.

**Documented access.** Every tool or data source an agent can access must be documented as part of the agent's specification. This is required for audit and change management purposes.

**No credentials in prompts.** API keys, passwords, and access tokens must never appear in prompts, skill instructions, or workflow configurations. Use approved secrets management practices.

